<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yakov Sverdlov</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1885">1885</a>–<a href="/years/1919">1919</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yakov Sverdlov was a Bolshevik organiser and close associate of Lenin. From November 1917 he chaired the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/all-russian-central-executive-committee">All-Russian Central Executive Committee</a>, making him the formal head of state of Soviet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, while also running the party's secretariat. He was involved in the decision to kill the former tsar and his family in 1918 and died of illness in March 1919. Yekaterinburg was called Sverdlovsk after him from 1924 to 1991.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Sverdlov was born on 3 June 1885 (22 May O.S.) in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizhny-novgorod">Nizhny Novgorod</a> to a Jewish family active in revolutionary politics; his father was an engraver who forged documents and stored arms for the underground. After four years of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gymnasium">gymnasium</a> he left to become a pharmacist's apprentice, joined the Russian Social Democratic Labour Party in 1902 and supported Lenin's Bolshevik faction from 1903. He took part in the 1905 revolution in the Urals. From his arrest in June 1906 until 1917 he was mostly imprisoned or exiled, and in 1914-1916 he was in internal exile in Turukhansk, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a>, along with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-stalin">Joseph Stalin</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After the February Revolution Sverdlov returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petrograd">Petrograd</a>, met Lenin in April 1917 and in August was elected one of five members of the Central Committee's Secretariat. He helped plan the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/october-revolution">October Revolution</a> and the decision to stage an armed uprising. In November 1917 he was elected chairman of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/all-russian-central-executive-committee">All-Russian Central Executive Committee</a>, the de facto head of state, and in March 1918 chairman of the Secretariat. He played roles in the dissolution of the Constituent Assembly in January 1918 and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-brest-litovsk">Treaty of Brest-Litovsk</a>, signed on 3 March. His organizing ability was well regarded, and thousands of local party committees were started during his chairmanship.</p><p>Sverdlov supported the Red Terror. After the assassination of Moisei Uritsky and the attempt on Lenin's life in August 1918 he drafted a document calling for merciless mass terror against the enemies of the revolution, and he took over some of Lenin's duties during his recovery. He also backed decossackization, which killed thousands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossacks</a>. Many sources claim that he, with Lenin and Filipp Goloshchyokin, played a major part in the execution of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicholas-ii">Nicholas II</a> and his family on 17 July 1918, and Trotsky's diaries record Sverdlov telling him &quot;We decided it here.&quot;</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Sverdlov died on 16 March 1919, aged 33, most commonly attributed to the Spanish flu contracted after a visit to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> and Oryol, and was buried in the Kremlin Wall Necropolis. Mikhail Kalinin eventually succeeded him as chairman of the Central Executive Committee. Some historians see his early death as a factor in Stalin's rise, since Sverdlov was a natural candidate for the post of General Secretary. Yekaterinburg was renamed Sverdlovsk in 1924 and regained its name in 1991.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Yakov Sverdlov” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Formally, supreme power belonged to the All-Russian Congress of Soviets and its Central Executive Committee, chaired by <b>Yakov Sverdlov</b> and, from 1919, Mikhail Kalinin, with the Sovnarkom as the government.</blockquote><a href="/empire/russian-sfsr">Russian SFSR · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizhny-novgorod">Nizhny Novgorod</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=56.33,44.01&amp;year=1907" aria-label="Nizhny Novgorod on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5504 248 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Yakov Sverdlov"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5954 518h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5954 518h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=56.33,44.01&amp;year=1907">What was here in 1907? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
